1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 APR 1723
2 ADDR
3 CITY Town of Killingworth
3 STAE Connecticut Colony
3 CTRY British America, Kingdom of Great Britain
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 DEC 1760
2 ADDR
3 STAE Province of Massachusetts Bay
3 CTRY British America, Kingdom of Great Britain
1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''NOTE''': Prior to May 1785, Killingworth was part of New London County, Connecticut. Thereafter, Killingworth became part of the newly created Middlesex County, Connecticut.
2 CONT * Peleg Redfield, fifth son of THEOPHILUS (8), son of James  
2 CONT * (4), resided at Killingworth. He married, April 25, 1744, Sarah Dudley, bom April 20, 1723, daughter of Ebenezer and Elizabeth [Graves] Dudley, of Guilford.  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Peleg's birth, military, and death information are available on pages 13, 14, 18, and 19, sections 8 and 18, of ''Genealogical History of the Redfield Family in the United States'', by John Howard Redfield. Albany, N.Y.: Munsell
2 CONC  & Rowland, 1860. Available online at https://books.google.com/books?id=Q3hMAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA255&dq=Osbert+Sidney+Comstock&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiZluPLpcuDAxXCrokEHcXpAooQ6AF6BAgKEAI#v=onepage&q=Rufus&f=false
2 CONT ** In 1756, Peleg was appointed a second lieutenant in the 10th Company of the Second Connecticut Regiment. the regiment was raised for a campaign against Frenc possessions in North America. In 1758, Peleg served as a first lieute
2 CONC nant in the 10th Company, in the British campaign against Fort Ticonderoga (which was lost), and in which Lord Howe was killed. 
2 CONT ** In the spring of 1759, Peleg received a commission as a captain. He took command of a 90-man company, which he raised in and around Killingworth. His company was part of a 5,000-man contingent from Connecticut, which was raised
2 CONC  that year to protect the Connecticut Colony during the war against French Canada. Captain Redfield's company was in the Second Connecticut Regiment, commanded by Major-General Lyman; the regiment was part of the division commande
2 CONC d by General Amherst.
