1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for CONNECTICUT with the rank of NON-COMMISSIONED OFFICER.  [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A094127  DAR Ancestor #  A09412]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Levi's estimate year of birth, military information, and death information are available on pages 34 and 35, section 56, of ''Genealogical History of the Redfield Family in the United States'', by John Howard Redfield. Albany, N
2 CONC .Y.: Munsell & Rowland, 1860. Available online at https://books.google.com/books?id=Q3hMAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA255&dq=Osbert+Sidney+Comstock&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiZluPLpcuDAxXCrokEHcXpAooQ6AF6BAgKEAI#v=onepage&q=Ru
2 CONC fus&f=false
2 CONT * Levi participated in two military campaigns:
2 CONT ** "In 1762, at the age of 17, Levi enlisted at Killingworth in the Connecticut regiment commanded by Colonel Putnam. This regiment joined British infantry and naval forces on an attack on Spanish possessions in the West Indies. "
2 CONC The Connecticut forces consisted of 1,000 men, to which were joined 800 men from New York, and 500 from New Jersey, the whole commanded by Gen. Lyman. The expedition sailed from New York in June 1762. Off the coast of Cuba, a stor
2 CONC m dispersed the fleet, and the transport, which carried Col. Putnam and 500 men, was wrecked upon an uninhabited key. They were afterwards relieved and reached Havana in time to render essential aid in the conduct of the siege of 
2 CONC that city. The regular forces had been engaged in the siege for some weeks and were worn down with fatigue and sickness. The castle and city of Havana soon after capitulated, and in November, the provincial forces, or rather a sad
2 CONC  remnant of them, returned to New York, the greater portion having perished from exposure and hardship, and consequent illness...Levi's elder brother, Nathan, died from fever on the homeward voyage, and he himself suffered from th
2 CONC e disease."
2 CONT ** "On the breaking out of the revolution, he enlisted in the Continental service, and served through several campaigns as brigade drum major. After the war he resumed his profession of singing-master, removed into Vermont in 1784
2 CONC , and lived mostly in Battleboro, though travelling often into other parts of the state, in exercise of his vocation. He afterwards removed to Otsego, Otsego County, N.Y., and thence to Bainbridge, N.Y., where he died September 15
2 CONC , 1838, aged 93 years."
