1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Heman Judd Redfield'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heman_J._Redfield
2 CONT * https://books.google.com/books?id=nhAbAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA400&lpg=PA400&dq=senator+heman+redfield&source=bl&ots=MWIBNpQ3Nm&sig=9pLCcdwKsXSS86iDr6cDtSRS4D4&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi_8rum98rMAhWBZiYKHQSCAqQQ6AEILDAC#v=onepage&q=senator%20
2 CONC heman%20redfield&f=false
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/92421243/heman_judd_redfield
2 CONT * http://www.buffalonian.com/hnews/1889REARADMIRALCHANDLER.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Redfield was an American politician from New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Life''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Peleg Redfield (1762–1852) and Mary (Judd) Redfield (1765–1844). The family were neighbors of Oliver Phelps who opened a land sales office in Suffield, Connecticut, after the Phelps and Gorham Purchase. The Redfi
2 CONC elds exchanged their small property in Suffield for 200 acres (0.81 km2) of land in the Town of Farmington (in that part that was later separated as Manchester, Ontario County, New York) and moved to the West in 1800.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended Canandaigua Academy from 1808 to 1810, and then studied law with John Canfield Spencer. During the War of 1812, he enlisted as a private and fought in the Battle of Queenston Heights, and in November 1813 at Fort Georg
2 CONC e, Ontario where he received from the commanding general William Henry Harrison a brevet for gallant services.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was admitted to the bar in 1815, and commenced practice at Le Roy. On January 27, 1817, he married Abigail N. Gould (d. 1841), and they had 14 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed District Attorney of Genesee County in 1821. He was Postmaster of Le Roy for more than twenty years. He was a member of the New York State Senate from 1823 to 1825.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1826, he was offered the position of Special Counsel to the New York State Attorney General for the trials against the abductors of William Morgan, but declined, recommending John Canfield Spencer who was chosen.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 9, 1835, Redfield was elected a Canal Commissioner by the New York State Legislature to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of Michael Hoffman, but he declined to take office. The Legislature adjourned on May 11, and
2 CONC  left Governor William L. Marcy to appoint John Bowman instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Holland Land Company sold out their land in 1836, he became land agent for the new proprietors and moved to Batavia, NY.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 14, 1846, he married Constance C. Bolles (d. 1909), and they had four children. President Franklin Pierce appointed Redfield Naval Officer of the Port of New York, and on November 1, 1853, Collector of the Port of New Yor
2 CONC k. He resigned on July 1, 1857, and retired from politics.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * https://www.ancestry.com/genealogy/records/herman-judd-redfield_19661808
2 CONT * https://www.ourfamtree.org/browse.php/Herman-Judd-Redfield/p569036
2 CONT * http://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Alexander_Redfield_(2)
2 CONT * http://radaris.com/p/Heman/Redfield/
