1 NAME Mary /Cooper/
2 GIVN Mary
2 SURN Cooper
2 _MARNM Burnett
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 AUG 1621
2 ADDR
3 CITY Olney
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 JUL 1706
2 ADDR
3 CITY Southampton
3 STAE New York
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mary Cooper was baptized on 12 Aug 1621 in Olney, Buckinghamshire, daughter of John Cooper and Wibroe Griggs.  She emigrated on the Hopewell, 1635, to Lynn, Massachusetts Bay Colony with her parents. She was not na
2 CONC med in her father's 1662 will.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===disputed origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/burnett/4547/
2 CONT
2 CONT >So either Mary Cooper had died prior to her fathers death in 1662 but with no record, or, she was alive but for some reason left out of her father will. But if left out of her father will, why? Was it something she her husband ha
2 CONC d done? I searched the records again for all mentions of Thomas Burnet and found something very interesting. According to the First Book of Records of the Town of Southampton (Hedges, Pelletreau, Foster, 1874) there are at least t
2 CONC hree incidents that ended up in court between John Cooper and Thomas Burnet. On two of those occasions, John Cooper was charged with battery on the person of Thomas Burnet. Battery was an almost unheard of occurrence in puritan So
2 CONC uthampton so this seems significant. Clearly Deacon Cooper had a serious issue with Thomas Burnet. None of the records gave a reason why he attacked Thomas Burnet, just that he did. Could they have been father and son-in-law? Thom
2 CONC as and Mary Burnet did name their first son John. Could this possibly have been after John Cooper? The question is then was John Cooper dislike of Thomas Burnet cause enough for him to leave his daughter Mary and her children out 
2 CONC of his will, assuming they were married?
2 CONT
2 CONT >In the book: The Records for the Court of Sessions of Southampton County in the Province of New York  1670-1688, by Thomas W. Cooper, Heritage Books, 1993, pages 73-75, is the inventory of John Cooper, Jr., Mary's brother, taken 
2 CONC when he died intestate some 15 years after his father. In that inventory is an item, the only one without a value; "one bed ofMaryes given her by her father". This inventory was taken by five people, one of whom was Thomas Cooper,
2 CONC  brother to John (and Mary). This is also the only item in the inventory showing ownership of an item by someone other than the deceased John. If Mary had been deceased before her brother, why identify the bed as belonging to Mary
2 CONC ? A reasonable interpretation is that John had been holding this bed for his sister Mary. If so, Mary was alive in 1677. This also further supports the idea that there was some longstanding disagreement between Mary Cooper and her
2 CONC  father John Cooper in that she had not (as of her brother death) yet accepted the bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT >There is still another account from the records of Southampton showing that Henry Pierson, the town clerk, was directed to act on behalf of the family of Thomas Burnet and required any purchase Thomas made of over 5 had to be app
2 CONC roved by him. Apparently Thomas was more generous with his money than his peers thought he should be. There is no indication of how long Henry Pierson overseeing of Thomas spending lasted, but it the only incident of its kind in t
2 CONC he early records. It seems to indicate a special relationship between Henry Pierson and Thomas Burnet. If they were brothers-in-law Henry would be looking out for his sister family and their assets which, based on Thomas will were
2 CONC  significant. Thomas had done fairly well for his family in Southampton. How much of that is attributable to Henry Pierson is unclear but may have been a factor. Henry died in 1680, just four years before Thomas did.
2 CONT
2 CONT >In summary, I thought it more than coincidental that Howell had mistakenly misplaced two Mary Piersons, the  first who Thomas Burnet did not marry in 1663 and the second Mary Pierson (he presumed was Mary Coopers married name) wh
2 CONC o did not marry Henry Pierson (she could not have). I also have a nagging feeling that among the bits and pieces Howell collected for his history was one he missed, misinterpreted, or second-guessed. That being that Mary, if a sis
2 CONC ter to Henry Pierson could easily have been assumed to have been surnamed Pierson, especially if the original source was unaware they were half-siblings. Otherwise why was Howell looking 600 miles away from Southampton to a place 
2 CONC Thomas Burnet had not been for 20 years for a Mary Pierson when there was a Mary (Pierson) Cooper in Southampton right under his nose? But there are more parts to this puzzle.Mary Cooper was unaccounted for in early Southampton re
2 CONC cords as Mary Cooper. She was not mentioned in her fathers will but was mentioned in her brother will in 1677. There is the fact that Thomas Burnet and John Cooper had some longstanding disagreement. Then Henry Pierson was oversee
2 CONC ing Thomas' spending. Each of these indirect (with regards to Mary Cooper) pieces of evidence could easily have more than one explanation, but collectively they all have one possible explanation in common - that Mary Cooper was th
2 CONC e wife of Thomas Burnet. All the above sources are quoted so that if you or anyone else wants to follow up on them you can. I may also be able to help in cases where you can find a particular source locally as I have copies of mos
2 CONC t of them here. Any questions or comments are welcome.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000076355084856& "John Cooper"] in The Great Migration Begins
2 CONT * [https://s3.amazonaws.com/photos.geni.com/p13/cd/33/42/c6/53444843aebc19b2/thomas_burnett_jr_genealogy_original.jpg Thomas Burnett Jr Genealogy]
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=pf_GBAAAQBAJ&lpg=PA42&ots=H4LP-ttgLR&dq=thomas%20burnett%20mary%20cooper&pg=PA39#v=onepage&q&f=false page 42] of Burnet - Ferguson - Schneider by Jim Schneider Lulu.com, Mar 19, 2013 - History 
2 CONC - 374 pages
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT @R600612987@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=111848078&pid=554 
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/196686492/mary-burnett
2 CONT
2 CONT Only wife of Thomas Burnett and daughter of Wibroe Griggs and John Cooper. Thomas did not marry another woman named Mary Pierson, the Mary Pierson and Thomas Burnitt, Thomas's (actual surname was Burnap, misspelled on church recor
2 CONC d), who married in Lynn MA in 1663 have been proven by their descendants, and to not be this Mary Cooper and attached husband Thomas Burnett. This should be obvious, since this Thomas and Mary were living in The Hamptons at that t
2 CONC ime.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary arrived at Massachusetts Bay Colony on the ship Hopewell in 1635 with her parent's and siblings, John, Thomas and Martha. Her oldest sister Ann and half-brother Henry Pierson arrived in America at a later date.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I haven't seen a document recording the exact date of death for Mary or her husband Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I've attached the following article, which shows her mother's two marriages and her children from each marriage, plus their spouses, which I can't confirm, but I've corrected the birth and death dates with more recent findings.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *Contrary to previous genealogies, Thomas Cooper II proved conclusively in a TAG article in 1989 that Henry Pierson was not the husband of Wibroe Griggs daughter, Mary Cooper, as had been previously thought, but instead was the ch
2 CONC ild of Wibroe Griggs from a previous marriage to William Pierson. The identity of the wife of immigrant Henry Pierson, Mary _____, is unknown. Thus, Wibroe Griggs is the matriarch of two immigrant families in America; that of Henr
2 CONC y Pierson (her son) and John Cooper (her second husband).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT These are her two families, as shown in Olney, Buckinghamshire, England parish records. Some dates may be inaccurate.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st Marriage;
2 CONT
2 CONT Wibroe Griggs 1593 -
2 CONT
2 CONT +William Pierson 1577-1618
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Child;
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Pierson 1615-1680
2 CONT
2 CONT +Mary 1622-
2 CONT
2 CONT * There is a marriage record for a Mary Pierson of Southampton and Seth Fletcher, dated May 10, 1681. Seth is believed to be her second husband. Mary's will records the names of several children from her previous marriage. There i
