1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the son of https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Burnet-M-A-of-Cambridge/6000000002244359403 & https://www.geni.com/people/Jane-Burnett/6000000002322263598?through=6000000002244359403. It’s been disproved.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Thomas Burnet
2 CONT * Born between 1609 - 1622 in England 
2 CONT * Died abt 1684 in Southampton, New York 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Burnet was born in England. He married Mary Cooper.1 Thomas died circa 1684 in Southampton, Long Island, New York.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas and Mary Cooper
2 CONT # Lot Burnet1
2 CONT # Joel Burnet1
2 CONT # Lois Burnet1
2 CONT # Priscilla Burnet1
2 CONT # John Burnet1 b. 1645, d. 1694
2 CONT # Hester Burnet1 b. 1653, d. 5 Nov 1714
2 CONT # Aaron Burnet1 b. 1655
2 CONT # Miriam Burnet1 b. 1656, d. 1 Apr 1717
2 CONT # Dan Burnet1 b. 1664, d. 8 Jul 1729
2 CONT # Mordecai Burnet1 b. 1670, d. 1716
2 CONT # Matthias Burnet1 b. 1674, d. 4 Oct 1765
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was Mary Cooper, born August 12, 1621 in Olney, Buckinghamshire, England. As of now, no one has produced as marriage record; they may have married in Lynn, MA or Southampton, NY. Documents show Thomas was granted land in 
2 CONC Southampton, NY on October 13, 1643. Thomas and Mary had 11 children together. *See Thomas's will below; as it was written. Thomas predeceased his wife, no period documents recording the date of Thomas or Mary's death, or their bu
2 CONC rial locations have been found.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT 52. A Correction to the Ancestry of Thomas Burnet of Southampton, New York￼
2 CONT
2 CONT https://bolesbooksblog.wordpress.com/tag/new-york/
2 CONT
2 CONT October 26, 2021 ancestry, Bolesbooks, England, genealogy, Scotland Burnet, Burnett, Leys, Lincolnshire, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT In “40. The Scottish Background of the Southampton Burnet Family” I asserted that two descendants of Thomas Burnet (d. 1688), of Southampton, Long Island, New York, are genetically related to two descendants of Burnet lairds of Le
2 CONC ys (co. Kincardine) and of Crathes Castle (co. Aberdeen), Scotland.  '''That now appears to be in error.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Comparison of the Y-DNA tests of these two descendants to those of known descendants of Thomas Burnet of Southampton reveals that they are essentially unrelated. In fact they are of completely different haplogroups [1, 2]. '''Give
2 CONC n this outcome there can be little room for doubt that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was not a Leys descendant.''' ….
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT [1] I am indebted to Terry Barwin, volunteer genealogist for the “House of Burnett” (https://www.houseofburnett.org) for bringing these cases to my attention. They are described more fully in an article, “Burnett of Leys Paternal 
2 CONC Line Revealed”, coauthored by Terry and by Lawrence Pearse. It is available to subscribers of the House of Burnett website. A third case, also described in the article, is related to the other two, but his descent from the Leys Bu
2 CONC rnets has not been determined.
2 CONT
2 CONT [2] Some of the relevant Y-DNA results are available at https://www.familytreedna.com/public/Burnett. Compare “R1b-E Descendants of Thomas Burnett of Suffolk NY; sons Lot, Aaron, and Daniel” to “J-M172 Burnett of Leys; Kemnay Line
2 CONC , Ramsays of Balmain”.
2 CONT
2 CONT [3] In my earlier article I raised chronological questions about the 1609 christening, based on arguments that Thomas of Southampton’s first son John may have been b. ca 1648. Clearly, however, it is not impossible that he was 39 
2 CONC years of age at his first child’s birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://bolesbooksblog.wordpress.com/2019/01/06/40-the-scottish-background-of-the-southampton-burnet-family/
2 CONT
2 CONT 40. The Scottish Background of the Southampton Burnet Family
2 CONT
2 CONT January 6, 2019 dbboles ancestry, England, genealogy, New York, royalty, ScotlandAberdeenshire, Braintree, Burnet, Burnett, Crathes Castle, Essex, Gullett, Holbeach, Kincardineshire, Leys, Lincolnshire, Long Island, Southampton.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Please note that the Scottish portion of this post has been superseded by another post, namely “52. A Correction to the Ancestry of Thomas Burnet of Southampton, New York￼”.  '''It is concluded there that the Southampton family d
2 CONC oes not in fact descend from the Burnet of Leys family.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Burnet descent is fully described and referenced in the book The Omnibus Ancestry: 619 Documented American and European Lines.  It is available for download through Lulu.
2 CONT
2 CONT The patriarch of the Burnet family of Southampton, Long Island, NY, was Thomas Burnet, first known as a settler who arrived at Scout’s Bay, western Long Island, in 1640/1, and who died in Southampton in 1684 [1].  My wife descends
2 CONC  from him by way of her Gullett ancestors.  Thomas’ great-great-great-granddaughter Jane Burnett (1820/1-1883) married in 1849, as her second husband, Joshua Gullett (1825-1863), in Washington county, Indiana [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT For a number of years it has been claimed that Thomas of Southampton was the son of Thomas Burnet, a physician in Braintree, co. Essex, England, and grandson of Alexander Burnet, 11th laird of Leys, co. Kincardine, Scotland, a con
2 CONC nection explicitly stated in E. A. Bailey’s Crannog to Castle (2000) [3].  The laird, whose wife was Katherine Arbuthnot, has extensive traceable ancestry that includes a descent from King Robert III of Scotland [4]. …
2 CONT
2 CONT Grounds for Skepticism
2 CONT
2 CONT Much as I would like to trace a royal line of descent on behalf of my wife, I have been skeptical of the stated connection between the Southampton and Leys Burnetts.  There are three major grounds for skepticism, as follows.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. The reference given in Crannog to Castle for the Southampton to Leys connection was the “personal research notes” of a private individual. In absence of a description of the evidence, such notes cannot be assumed authoritative.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. There is little similarity between the names of the children of Thomas of Southampton (John, Lot, Hester, Joel, Aaron, Miriam, Priscilla, Daniel, Lois, Mordecai, and Matthias) and those of the children of Thomas of Braintree (T
2 CONC homas, Alexander, Frances), his wife (Jane), or his siblings (Alexander, Robert, Gilbert, Duncan, John, Janet, Margaret, and Elizabeth) [2]. It seems an important observation that the children’s names in the Southampton family app
2 CONC ear to be solidly English, while several names in the Braintree family are commonly Scottish. But in any case, the lack of resemblance between the two families is a reason to doubt that one closely derived from the other.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. There is also some question whether the chronology is consistent. The Braintree man did have a son named Thomas, who was b. 1612 [2]. However, the second child named in the Southampton man’s will, Lot, was m. 1675, and the thir
2 CONC d child, Hester, married a man b. 1649 [2].  These dates suggest that the first child, John (for whom no firm birth or marriage dates are otherwise known) was b. ca 1648? [6].  In turn that is suggestive that his father Thomas was
2 CONC  born later than 1612, possibly closer to 1622. …
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will of Thomas Barnett  written 1680
2 CONT *SUFFOLK COUNTY CLERK'S OFFICE SUFFOLK COUNTY SESSIONS, VOL. I, PAGE 161
2 CONT *WILL OF THOMAS BURNETT
2 CONT * In the name of God, Amen, I, Thomas Burnett, of Southampton, in ye east Riding of Yorkshire, in Long Island, husbandman, being sick of body but strong ye mercy of God, in perfect strength of memory, blessed be His name, for it a
2 CONC nd not knowing the day of my death doe make this my will and testament in manner and form following:
2 CONT * Imprimis, I give and bequeath my soul to Christ, my Redeemer, who by His blood hath purchased the same.
2 CONT * 1stly: I give my body to ye earth from whence it was first taken after a decent burial to be made by my executrix and for my wordly estate I give it as hereafter mentioned.
2 CONT * 2ndly: I give unto my six children, John, Joel, Lott, Hester, Merrium, Priscilla, each of them 5 shillings apiece, to be paid to them by my executrix.
2 CONT * 3rdly: I give unto my son, Aaron Burnett, my house and home lot after my wife's decease, and my 3 acres on ye 10 acre lott.
2 CONT * 4thly: And at my decease I give unto him my said son my ten acres lying at Meacoke Gate adjoining to ye land of Mr. Jonah Ffordham, all my ox pasture division, and 2 heifers and 2 steers, to be delivered to him by my executrix.
2 CONT * 5th1y: I give unto my two sons, Moredecai and Dan Burnett, all my land att ye place called Cormerants Point, to be equally divided between them when they shall obtain to ye age of 1 and 20 years only they shall summer 6 cows dur
2 CONC ing her life.
2 CONT * 6thly: I give unto my son, Matthias, after my said wife's decease, my leaf in ye Great Plains in Halseyes Neck.
2 CONT * 7thly: I give unto my daughter, Lois, at her day of Marriage, or at ye age of 18 which first happens, the sum of 10 pounds to be paid unto her by my executrix.
2 CONT * Lastly: I, by this my last will and testament, make my living wife, Mary Burnett sole executrix of this my last will and testament and to have all ye residue of my estate that is not disposed of, and to pay all my debts and lega
2 CONC cies herein- after mentioned.
2 CONT *In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal, this the 10th day of March 1679-80.
2 CONT *(signed) Thomas Burnett (Seal)
2 CONT *Witness; John Hester John Laughton
2 CONT *A true copy - John How Clark Dec. 2, 1689
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------
2 CONT "Note: The hyphenated date above , 1679-80, is the O.S. versus N.S. dating system. O.S. (Old Style) was in effect until about 1753 when the American colonies along with England changed to the N.S. system. Under the O.S., the year 
2 CONC ended on March 25th. Since the date in question falls within that time period when the two systems don't agree, it's hyphenated.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There are deeds on record from Thomas Burnet to several of his sons, showing that he had liberally provided for them, before making the bequest of five shillings to each." -Paul Burnet at www.garlic.com
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://s3.amazonaws.com/photos.geni.com/p13/cd/33/42/c6/53444843aebc19b2/thomas_burnett_jr_genealogy_original.jpg Thomas Burnett Jr Genealogy]
2 CONT
2 CONT >....In addition, the research of William Burnet Austin, also stated that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was the son of Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree, Essex, England. Dr. Thomas Burnet is, in turn, the son of Alexander Burnet, 11th
2 CONC  of Leys and his wife Katherine Arbuthnot of Fiddes. This same Arbuthnot family connection, as it turns out, provides a direct line of descent from most of the lines of Kings of Europe and Asia.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Many early records also stated that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was related to Gilbert Burnet, Bishop of Salsbury, and his son, Governor William Burnet, Colonial Governor of NJ, NY, and MA. Since Bishop Burnet was a member of th
2 CONC e Burnet of Leys family, this also makes our Thomas Burnet a descendant of the Burnets of Leys. In researching the book "The Family of Burnett of Leys, With Collateral Branches," by George Burnett, LL.D., Lyon King of Arms, 1901, 
2 CONC there are 15 Thomas Burnet(t)s listed. All but one can be accounted for--Thomas Burnet, the son of Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree. Also, in the same book, the connection is made between Bishop Gilbert Burnet and the same Thomas Bu
2 CONC rnet, who is by all indications, our Thomas Burnet of Southampton. The book shows they were first cousins, once removed."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is a statement found in a History of Essex County, New Jersey:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"The Burnett family is probably as numerous as any other in the state and has always been very prominent in public affairs and in moulding those events which form the history of the Commonwealth. Liberal and public spirited, its 
2 CONC representatives largely promoted education, moral and material interests, and left the impress of their individuality upon public life."
2 CONT
2 CONT >Thomas Burnett, the first of this name in Southhampton, had a lot granted him October 13, 1643. He probably came here not long before this time. Savage mentions him as a resident of Lynn, Massachusetts, though Lewis and Newhall m
2 CONC ake no mention of him. No record of his emigration has been found to show from what county in England he came. the record of the branch in New Jersey has been furnished by Mr. John R. Burnett of South Orange, New Jersey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT from Lewis Compton Burnett, 1903: "(Thomas Burnett) was a Presbyterian Puritan and must have left England in the early 1630's. His second wife was Mary Pierson daughter of Rev. Pierson of Lynn Mass to whom he married in 1663. In t
2 CONC hese early years there were but few people in all of New England. So our ancestry dates from the very first to settle the new country. Lynn Mass was settled in 1630, 50 new settlers having come in that year from England. It was fi
2 CONC rst called Saugus. Named for Lynn Regis, England"
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographical Information   · 7 September 2014 · 0 Comments  From www.garlic.com/~pburnett/genealog.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Burnett's son Dan had a son Ichabod (b 1684, d 1774); Ichabod had a son William (b 1730); this William had a son David G. Burnett, who was the first president of the Republic of Texas in 1836. (from Lewis Compton Burnett, 1
2 CONC 903: "(Thomas Burnett) was a Presbyterian Puritan and must have left England in the early 1630's. His second wife was Mary Pierson daughter of Rev. Pierson of Lynn Mass to whom he married in 1663. In these early years there were b
2 CONC ut few people in all of New England. So our ancestry dates from the very first to settle the new country. Lynn Mass was settled in 1630, 50 new settlers having come in that year from England. It was first called Saugus. Named for 
2 CONC Lynn Regis, England" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Burnett, born in Scotland, emigrated to Massachusetts and removed to Southampton, Long Island, where he received his allotment of land October 6, 1643. Second marriage Dec. 3, 1663 at Lynn, Mass, Mary Pierson (Pearson) b. J
2 CONC une 20, 1643, daughter of John Pierson. Thomas by his first wife {Mary Cooper} had a son, Aaron, b. 1655" - Vigo County Historical Society, 1915; ref Volume I of the Genealogical and Memorial History of the State of New York, publ
2 CONC ished by the Lewis Historical Publishing Company, page 319. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT But  on the other hand: (from Jim Fina, 1999: "Nowhere, in any of the older histories of New England or Long Island, is any proof offered that Thomas Burnet of Southampton, came from Yorkshire--it seems to be based, not on researc
2 CONC h, but purely on conjecture. Nowhere does it mention his family, or who his father in Yorkshire might have been--just that he's supposed to have come from that part of England. Actually, Thomas Burnet, as later research shows, cam
2 CONC e from Braintree, Essex England. He removed to Lynn, MA, where he lived a short time, before relocating to Southampton, Long Island, NY. There, in 1643, he took a lot, according to Southampton Town Records. He remained there until
2 CONC  his death in 1684. He was married to Mary Cooper, and together they raised their 11 children, the first born in 1645. The noted genealogist Eugene Bouton, in doing research on Matthias Burnet, hired a professional genealogist, a 
2 CONC Mr. M.L. Hawley, who, in a letter dated February 1, 1932, reported that he had discovered the origins of this Long Island Burnet family. He stated that they were descended from Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree, Essex, England. In my
2 CONC  own researching, I discovered that Dr. Thomas Burnet had a son named Thomas, born in 1612, the oldest of three children. In addition, the research of William Burnet Austin, also stated that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was the so
2 CONC n of Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree, Essex, England. Dr. Thomas Burnet is, in turn, the son of Alexander Burnet, 11th of Leys and his wife Katherine Arbuthnot of Fiddes. This same Arbuthnot family connection, as it turns out, prov
2 CONC ides a direct line of descent from most of the lines of Kings of Europe and Asia. Many early records also stated that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was related to Gilbert Burnet, Bishop of Salsbury, and his son, Governor William Bu
2 CONC rnet, Colonial Governor of NJ, NY, and MA. Since Bishop Burnet was a member of the Burnet of Leys family, this also makes our Thomas Burnet a descendant of the Burnets of Leys. In researching the book "The Family of Burnett of Ley
2 CONC s, With Collateral Branches," by George Burnett, LL.D., Lyon King of Arms, 1901, there are 15 Thomas Burnet(t)s listed. All but one can be accounted for--Thomas Burnet, the son of Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree. Also, in the same 
2 CONC book, the connection is made between Bishop Gilbert Burnet and the same Thomas Burnet, who is by all indications, our Thomas Burnet of Southampton. The book shows they were first cousins, once removed."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Alexander Burnett (Jr.) [NO] arrived in Boston (Lynn/Manhasset at the head of Cow Bay ) in 1640
2 CONT
2 CONT His father: Thomas Burnet, M.A.[NO] 
2 CONT Also Known As: "Burnett"
2 CONT Birthdate: 1570
2 CONT Birthplace: Leys, Kincardineshire, Scotland
2 CONT Death: 1642 (70-72)
2 CONT Braintree, Essex, England
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st spouse: Mary Cooper ( Mary )
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. John
2 CONT 2. Joell
2 CONT 3. Lott
2 CONT 4. Hester
2 CONT 5. Merriam (Miriam )
2 CONT 6. Lois - m. Robert Collins
2 CONT 7. Mathais - Captain. m. Elizabeth Osborne
2 CONT 8. Mordecay
2 CONT 9. Dan Burnett (Thomas Burnett's son Dan had a son, Ichibold(b 1684, d 1774); Ichabod had a son, William (b 1730); this William had a son, David G. Burnett, who was the first president of the Republic of Texas in 1836.
2 CONT 10. Aaron b. 1655
2 CONT 11. Pricilla
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Burnett, the first of this name in Southampton, had a lot granted him October 13, 1643. He probably came here not long before this time. Savage mentions him as a resident of Lynn, Massachusetts, though Lewis and Newhall mak
2 CONC e no mention of him. No record of his emigration has been found to show from what county in England he came. The record of the branch in New Jersey has been furnished by Mr. John K. Burnett of South Orange, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Full text of "The early history of Southampton, L. I., New York" p. 206)
2 CONT
2 CONT from Lewis Compton Burnett, 1903: "(Thomas Burnett) was a Presbyterian Puritan and must have left England in the early 1630's. His second wife was Mary Pierson daughter of Rev. Pierson of Lynn Mass to whom he married in 1663. In t
2 CONC hese early years there were but few people in all of New England. So our ancestry dates from the very first to settle the new country. Lynn Mass was settled in 1630, 50 new settlers having come in that year from England. It was fi
2 CONC rst called Saugus. Named for Lynn Regis, England"
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT • He has conflicting death information of 1689. 3 12
2 CONT • He has conflicting death information of 26 May 1691 in Southampton, Long Island, Suffolk Co., New York. 3 13
2 CONT • He appeared on the census of 1683 in Southampton, Long Island, NY as Town assessment roll of inhabitants. The list is printed in the second volume of the Documentary History of New York, New York Genealogical & Biographical Soci
2 CONC ety Library.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
