1 CONT Thomas Burnett's Will - 1689
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT SUFFOLK COUNTY CLERK'S OFFICE
1 CONT SUFFOLK COUNTY SESSIONS, VOL. I, PAGE 161:
1 CONT
1 CONT WILL OF THOMAS BURNETT
1 CONT
1 CONT In the name of God Amen.
1 CONT
1 CONT I Thomas Burnett of Southampton in ye east Riding of Yorkshire on Long Island
1 CONT Husbandman being sick of body but through ye mercy of God in perfect strength of memory, 
1 CONT blessed be His name for it & not knowing the day of my death doe make this my will 
1 CONT and testament in manner and form following.
1 CONT
1 CONT Imprimis I give and bequeath my soule to Christ my redeemer who by His blood has 
1 CONT purchased the same.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2dly I give my body to ye Earth from whence it was first taken after a decent 
1 CONT buryale to be made by my Executrix and for my wordly estate I give it as hereafter 
1 CONT mentioned.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3dly John, Joell, Lott, Hester, Merrium, Priscilla each of them 5 shillings apiece 
1 CONT to be paid to them by my Executrix.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4thly I give unto my son Aaron Burnett my house and home lott after my wives decease 
1 CONT and my three acres lots in ye 10 acres lots and at my decease I give unto him my said 
1 CONT son my ten acres lying at Mecok's gate adjoining to ye land of Jonah Fordham and all 
1 CONT my ox pasture devission and two heifers and teo steers to be delivered to him by my 
1 CONT executrix.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5th1y I give unto my two sons Mordecay and Dan Burnet all my land at a place called 
1 CONT Cormerants point to be equally divided between them when they shall atain to age of 
1 CONT one and twenty years, only they shall summer six cows for my wife during her life.
1 CONT
1 CONT 6thly I give to my son Matthias after my said wive's decease my close in ye great 
1 CONT plains in Halsey Neck.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7thly I give unto my daughter Lois at her day of marriage or at ye age of eighteen 
1 CONT which shall first happen, ye sum of 10 pounds to be paid unto her by my executrix.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lastly I by this my last will and testament make my loving wife Mary Burnet sole 
1 CONT executrix of this my last will and testament and to have all ye residue of my estate 
1 CONT that is not disposed off and to pay all my debts and legacy as herein mentioned.
1 CONT
1 CONT In witness whereof I have hereunto sett my hand seale this sixteenth day of March 
1 CONT anno deam 1679.
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed, sealed and delivered in ye presence of
1 CONT
1 CONT John Ffoster
1 CONT
1 CONT John Laughton
1 CONT
1 CONT A true copyy December ye 2 1684
1 CONT
1 CONT Pr John Howell clerk
1 CONT
1 CONT Session Book #1, page 161.
1 CONT
1 CONT (As copied 28 August 1890 by O. B. Ackerly for B. W. Burnet. The spelling and punctuation, or lack thereof is as written by Mr. Ackerly.)
1 CONT
1 CONT 02-January-2006 - from the same website listed above, an interesting note:
1 CONT
1 CONT ...but on the other hand: (from Jim Fina, 1999: "Nowhere, in any of the older histories of New England or Long Island, is any proof offered that Thomas Burnet of Southampton, came from Yorkshire--it seems to be based, not on resea
1 CONC rch, but purely on conjecture. Nowhere does it mention his family, or who his father in Yorkshire might have been--just that he's supposed to have come from that part of England. Actually, Thomas Burnet, as later research shows, c
1 CONC ame from Braintree, Essex England. He removed to Lynn, MA, where he lived a short time, before relocating to Southampton, Long Island, NY. There, in 1643, he took a lot, according to Southampton Town Records. He remained there unt
1 CONC il his death in 1684. He was married to Mary Cooper, and together they raised their 11 children, the first born in 1645. The noted genealogist Eugene Bouton, in doing research on Matthias Burnet, hired a professional genealogist, 
1 CONC a Mr. M.L. Hawley, who, in a letter dated February 1, 1932, reported that he had discovered the origins of this Long Island Burnet family. He stated that they were descended from Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree, Essex, England. In 
1 CONC my own researching, I discovered that Dr. Thomas Burnet had a son named Thomas, born in 1612, the oldest of three children.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT       In addition, the research of William Burnet Austin, also stated that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was the son of Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree, Essex, England. Dr. Thomas Burnet is, in turn, the son of Alexander Burnet, 11t
1 CONC h of Leys and his wife Katherine Arbuthnot of Fiddes. This same Arbuthnot family connection, as it turns out, provides a direct line of descent from most of the lines of Kings of Europe and Asia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Many early records also stated that Thomas Burnet of Southampton was related to Gilbert Burnet, Bishop of Salsbury, and his son, Governor William Burnet, Colonial Governor of NJ, NY, and MA. Since Bishop Burnet was a member o
1 CONC f the Burnet of Leys family, this also makes our Thomas Burnet a descendant of the Burnets of Leys. In researching the book "The Family of Burnett of Leys, With Collateral Branches," by George Burnett, LL.D., Lyon King of Arms, 19
1 CONC 01, there are 15 Thomas Burnet(t)s listed. All but one can be accounted for--Thomas Burnet, the son of Dr. Thomas Burnet of Braintree. Also, in the same book, the connection is made between Bishop Gilbert Burnet and the same Thoma
1 CONC s Burnet, who is by all indications, our Thomas Burnet of Southampton. The book shows they were first cousins, once removed." 
